{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-223120",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\  & =-2⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =|-2|\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
